We can use the product-to-sum identities to simplify the expression.
\[\sin 6^\circ \sin 42^\circ \sin 66^\circ \sin 78^\circ = \frac{1}{2} \left( \cos 36^\circ - \cos 48^\circ \right) \left( \cos 12^\circ - \cos 24^\circ \right) \left( \cos 24^\circ - \cos 42^\circ \right) \left( \cos 12^\circ + \cos 78^\circ \right).\]
Expanding and rearranging terms, we get
\begin{align*}
&\frac{1}{2} \left( \cos 36^\circ \cos 12^\circ \cos 24^\circ \cos 78^\circ - \cos 36^\circ \cos 12^\circ \cos 24^\circ \cos 42^\circ \right) \\
&\quad - \frac{1}{2} \left( \cos 48^\circ \cos 12^\circ \cos 24^\circ \cos 78^\circ - \cos 48^\circ \cos 12^\circ \cos 24^\circ \cos 42^\circ \right).
\end{align*}
By the double angle and half angle identities, we can simplify this expression further.
The final result is $\boxed{\frac{1}{16}}$.
The answer is: \frac{1}{16}

