solution_steps:
We can write $\cos x + 2 \sin x$ as $\sqrt{5} \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \cos x + \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \sin x \right)$.
By the angle addition formula,
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \cos x + \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \sin x &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} (\cos x + 2 \sin x) \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \left( \sqrt{5} \cos x \cos 30^\circ + \sqrt{5} \sin x \sin 30^\circ \right) \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \sqrt{5} (\cos x \cos 30^\circ + \sin x \sin 30^\circ) \\
&= \cos (x - 30^\circ).
\end{align*}
Since $-1 \le \cos (x - 30^\circ) \le 1$ for all angles $x$, the maximum value of $\cos x + 2 \sin x$ is $\boxed{\sqrt{5}}$.
The answer is: \sqrt{5}

